1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,656
That question reaches you from a team
lead. Two more arrive the same week.

2
00:00:08,696 --> 00:00:13,193
"Production falls over on an empty question;
I need a fix in ten minutes", while

3
00:00:13,233 --> 00:00:17,786
you're in the middle of a rebase that has
stopped at a conflict, where git switch

4
00:00:17,826 --> 00:00:22,436
main is refused and git stash fails. And
"can you look at Asha's pull request now,

5
00:00:22,476 --> 00:00:27,142
she is blocked", while your working tree
holds two hours of half-finished edits and

6
00:00:27,182 --> 00:00:28,220
a warm build cache.

7
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:32,609
The usual answers are a second clone,
or a stash and a branch switch.

8
00:00:32,649 --> 00:00:37,410
A second clone costs a full copy of the
object database and has to be fetched and

9
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:42,388
kept in sync separately. A stash and a switch
disturb the one working tree you have,

10
00:00:42,428 --> 00:00:45,944
and during a conflicted rebase
they aren't available at all.

11
00:00:46,284 --> 00:00:51,086
Git's own answer is a linked worktree: one
more working tree, a directory of files

12
00:00:51,126 --> 00:00:55,633
you edit, with its own HEAD and its own
index, attached to the repository you

13
00:00:55,673 --> 00:01:00,121
already have. HEAD says where you are,
and the index holds your next commit.

14
00:01:00,161 --> 00:01:01,834
Keep the four agents in mind.

15
00:01:03,674 --> 00:01:07,444
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

16
00:01:07,484 --> 00:01:12,628
You used a worktree twice already without
studying it: once to show a detached HEAD

17
00:01:12,668 --> 00:01:17,125
on a tag, and once to test a suspect
commit and its parent side by side.

18
00:01:17,165 --> 00:01:22,059
Today you learn the mechanism. What is
created on disk, what is shared, what is

19
00:01:22,099 --> 00:01:26,057
private, and the one rule that
keeps the whole arrangement safe.

20
00:01:26,097 --> 00:01:28,118
The next video is about using it.

21
00:01:28,458 --> 00:01:32,626
The project is rag-api, a retrieval-augmented
question-answering service.

22
00:01:32,666 --> 00:01:36,372
main is the release line and
feature/rerank is your topic branch.

23
00:01:38,212 --> 00:01:42,552
say what git worktree add creates in the
new directory and in the repository;

24
00:01:42,892 --> 00:01:45,965
list what worktrees share and
what each has for itself;

25
00:01:46,305 --> 00:01:51,126
find the real location of a per-worktree
file with git rev-parse --git-path;

26
00:01:51,466 --> 00:01:56,529
explain why Git refuses to check out one
branch in two worktrees, and what forcing

27
00:01:56,569 --> 00:01:57,151
it breaks.

28
00:01:58,991 --> 00:02:04,130
In one sentence: 🟢 SAFE, git worktree add
<path> <branch> creates a second directory

29
00:02:04,170 --> 00:02:09,186
of checked-out files that belongs to the
same repository: the same objects and the

30
00:02:09,226 --> 00:02:12,084
same refs, with a HEAD
and an index of its own.

31
00:02:12,424 --> 00:02:17,531
Precisely. The manual distinguishes the
main worktree, the one created by git init

32
00:02:17,571 --> 00:02:21,549
or git clone, from linked worktrees
created by git worktree add.

33
00:02:21,589 --> 00:02:26,508
A repository has one main worktree, unless
it is bare, and any number of linked

34
00:02:26,548 --> 00:02:31,467
ones. The word "worktree" means the working
tree together with its per-worktree

35
00:02:31,507 --> 00:02:32,032
metadata.

36
00:02:32,372 --> 00:02:37,535
Inside the dot git folder, two things are
created. In the new directory there is a

37
00:02:37,575 --> 00:02:42,168
.git file, not a directory, that
contains one line pointing back into the

38
00:02:42,208 --> 00:02:47,308
repository. In the repository there is a
private directory, .git/worktrees/<id>/,

39
00:02:47,348 --> 00:02:52,448
that holds what belongs to this worktree
alone: HEAD, index, logs/HEAD, ORIG_HEAD

40
00:02:52,488 --> 00:02:57,589
and the other root refs, plus two small
files. gitdir says where the working tree

41
00:02:57,629 --> 00:03:02,158
is. commondir says where the shared
part is, relative to this directory.

42
00:03:02,498 --> 00:03:06,534
So inside a linked worktree Git
works with two directories.

43
00:03:06,574 --> 00:03:11,371
$GIT_DIR is the private one.
$GIT_COMMON_DIR is the repository's .git.

44
00:03:11,411 --> 00:03:15,724
Every path under "the Git directory"
belongs to one of the two.

45
00:03:16,064 --> 00:03:20,016
Try it now, on paper. Per
worktree, or shared? HEAD.

46
00:03:20,056 --> 00:03:26,311
The index. The objects. Branches. The stash.
Pause me for thirty seconds and write

47
00:03:26,351 --> 00:03:27,232
your answer.

48
00:03:32,072 --> 00:03:37,178
HEAD and the index are on the left, per
worktree. Objects, branches and the stash

49
00:03:37,218 --> 00:03:38,766
are on the right, shared.

50
00:03:39,106 --> 00:03:44,402
The manual's rule is short: refs that start
with refs/ are shared, with the three

51
00:03:44,442 --> 00:03:49,739
exceptions in the last row of the left
column, and root refs such as HEAD are per

52
00:03:49,779 --> 00:03:54,153
worktree. It also warns not to
guess. Ask git rev-parse --git-path.

53
00:03:54,493 --> 00:03:59,593
Now the rule. In one sentence: a branch can
be checked out in at most one worktree,

54
00:03:59,633 --> 00:04:02,814
and Git refuses every command
that would break this.

55
00:04:03,154 --> 00:04:07,880
Why? A branch is one ref. A worktree that
has the branch checked out assumes that

56
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:11,588
its index and its files correspond
to the commit the ref names.

57
00:04:11,628 --> 00:04:16,531
If two worktrees shared the ref, a commit
in one would move the ref under the other,

58
00:04:16,571 --> 00:04:21,474
whose index and files would stay where they
were. On screen is that second worktree,

59
00:04:21,514 --> 00:04:23,652
with the IDs you'll meet in the demo.

60
00:04:23,992 --> 00:04:28,331
When you need the files of a branch that
is checked out elsewhere, and you don't

61
00:04:28,371 --> 00:04:32,930
intend to commit to it, detach. A detached
worktree holds a commit, not a branch, so

62
00:04:32,970 --> 00:04:35,448
any number of them can
sit on the same commit.

63
00:04:35,788 --> 00:04:39,168
Two things count as "checked
out" that are often missed.

64
00:04:39,208 --> 00:04:44,297
A branch that is in the middle of a rebase
or a bisect in some worktree is in use by

65
00:04:44,337 --> 00:04:48,816
that worktree, although git worktree
list shows that worktree as detached.

66
00:04:48,856 --> 00:04:53,641
And git rebase --update-refs skips branches
that are checked out in a worktree.

67
00:04:55,481 --> 00:04:58,380
The textbook's analogy: one
library, several desks.

68
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:03,164
Every desk has its own open book and its
own bookmark: the checked-out files, HEAD,

69
00:05:03,204 --> 00:05:08,006
the index. The shelves, which are the objects,
and the catalogue, which is the refs,

70
00:05:08,046 --> 00:05:12,675
are shared. A book that one desk adds to
the shelves is on the shelves for all of

71
00:05:12,715 --> 00:05:12,963
them.

72
00:05:13,303 --> 00:05:18,206
The analogy breaks at one rule that libraries
don't have: Git allows a given branch

73
00:05:18,246 --> 00:05:19,873
to be open at one desk only.

74
00:05:20,213 --> 00:05:23,259
Hold on to the two halves
when you predict behavior.

75
00:05:23,299 --> 00:05:27,651
Anything that is an object or a ref
under refs/ is visible from every desk

76
00:05:27,691 --> 00:05:32,577
immediately, with no fetch and no push. On
screen, a commit made at the other desk:

77
00:05:32,617 --> 00:05:37,562
feature/rerank moves here too. Anything that
is "where I am" or "what I have staged"

78
00:05:37,602 --> 00:05:38,749
belongs to one desk.

79
00:05:40,589 --> 00:05:45,133
Read the three small files as a pair of
pointers. The .git file in the linked

80
00:05:45,173 --> 00:05:49,003
working tree says where this
worktree's private Git directory is.

81
00:05:49,043 --> 00:05:52,635
gitdir inside that directory
points back at the working tree.

82
00:05:52,675 --> 00:05:57,397
That's how git worktree list and git
worktree prune find out whether the working

83
00:05:57,437 --> 00:06:02,339
tree still exists. commondir contains two
dots, slash, two dots: two levels up from

84
00:06:02,379 --> 00:06:05,137
the private directory is
.git, the shared part.

85
00:06:06,977 --> 00:06:12,117
"Preparing worktree (checking out
'feature/rerank')" is an ordinary checkout into

86
00:06:12,157 --> 00:06:17,233
another directory. No objects were copied.
git worktree list prints one line per

87
00:06:17,273 --> 00:06:22,093
worktree: path, the commit its HEAD
resolves to, and the branch in brackets.

88
00:06:22,133 --> 00:06:24,268
The main worktree is always first.

89
00:06:24,608 --> 00:06:28,463
Predict: in the new directory,
is .git a directory?

90
00:06:28,503 --> 00:06:30,525
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

91
00:06:42,865 --> 00:06:47,784
A file, one line, beginning with gitdir:.
And inside the repository, the private

92
00:06:47,824 --> 00:06:51,875
directory with HEAD, index, logs,
ORIG_HEAD, gitdir and commondir.

93
00:06:51,915 --> 00:06:53,115
That is the diagram.

94
00:06:53,455 --> 00:06:57,281
Now ask Git where things are,
from inside the linked worktree.

95
00:06:57,621 --> 00:07:00,908
The Git directory is the
private one under worktrees.

96
00:07:00,948 --> 00:07:06,120
The common directory is the repository's
.git. HEAD, index, logs/HEAD and ORIG_HEAD

97
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:11,269
resolve into the private directory. The
rest of the snippet shows the shared paths

98
00:07:11,309 --> 00:07:16,041
resolving into the common one. In a
script, never build such a path by hand.

99
00:07:16,381 --> 00:07:21,527
First consequence. Commit in the linked
worktree, then look from the main one.

100
00:07:21,567 --> 00:07:26,779
Quick quiz, three options. To see that
commit here you need a fetch, a push, or

101
00:07:26,819 --> 00:07:28,441
nothing. Say it out loud.

102
00:07:33,281 --> 00:07:38,118
Nothing. No fetch, no push: the branch
ref and the new objects are already here,

103
00:07:38,158 --> 00:07:43,179
because they are shared. In git branch -v
the asterisk marks the branch checked out

104
00:07:43,219 --> 00:07:47,446
here and the plus sign marks a branch
checked out in another worktree.

105
00:07:47,786 --> 00:07:50,426
Second consequence: staging is private.

106
00:07:50,766 --> 00:07:55,772
A file staged in the main worktree doesn't
appear in the status of the linked one.

107
00:07:55,812 --> 00:07:56,511
Two indexes.

108
00:07:56,851 --> 00:08:02,071
And because root refs are per worktree,
the name HEAD means something different in

109
00:08:02,111 --> 00:08:05,856
each directory. To name another
worktree's HEAD, prefix it.

110
00:08:15,996 --> 00:08:19,142
"'main' is already used by
worktree at" and the path.

111
00:08:19,182 --> 00:08:23,772
Both ways are refused. The same rule
protects a checked-out branch from being

112
00:08:23,812 --> 00:08:25,696
deleted or moved from elsewhere.

113
00:08:26,036 --> 00:08:28,993
The supported way to get the
same files without the branch:

114
00:08:29,333 --> 00:08:33,740
Two worktrees on commit 323e8f1,
one on the branch and one detached.

115
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:37,504
Now see once, in a sandbox,
what the rule prevents.

116
00:08:37,544 --> 00:08:40,627
--force lets you build
exactly that situation.

117
00:08:42,967 --> 00:08:47,345
You force a second checkout of main,
then commit in the first worktree.

118
00:08:47,385 --> 00:08:51,390
Nobody touches the second. Predict
what git status reports there.

119
00:08:51,730 --> 00:08:54,948
"Changes to be committed", in
a directory nobody edited.

120
00:08:54,988 --> 00:08:55,763
Which changes?

121
00:08:56,103 --> 00:08:59,357
The staged diff removes the lines
that the latest commit added.

122
00:08:59,697 --> 00:09:04,441
A git commit in the stale worktree at this
point would record the old tree on top of

123
00:09:04,481 --> 00:09:08,256
the new commit: a silent revert of
a colleague's work, or your own.

124
00:09:08,296 --> 00:09:10,876
That's the failure the
rule exists to prevent.

125
00:09:11,216 --> 00:09:16,374
The repair. 🔴 DANGEROUS: git reset --hard
overwrites the index and the working tree

126
00:09:16,414 --> 00:09:21,072
and destroys uncommitted changes to
tracked files. Preview with git status.

127
00:09:21,112 --> 00:09:25,269
It's appropriate here because this
worktree had no work of its own.

128
00:09:31,609 --> 00:09:33,257
Five mistakes to watch for.

129
00:09:33,597 --> 00:09:38,642
Expecting to fetch or push between two
worktrees. Root cause: objects and all refs

130
00:09:38,682 --> 00:09:43,168
under refs/ are shared; a commit made
in one exists in the other at once.

131
00:09:43,508 --> 00:09:49,207
Building a path such as .git/index by
hand in a script that runs in a linked

132
00:09:49,247 --> 00:09:54,341
worktree. Root cause: there .git is
a file and the index lives under

133
00:09:54,381 --> 00:09:59,702
.git/worktrees/<id>/; git rev-parse
--git-path gives the real location.

134
00:10:00,042 --> 00:10:04,125
git worktree add --force to get
a second checkout of a branch.

135
00:10:04,165 --> 00:10:09,446
Root cause: one ref and two indexes; a
commit in one worktree leaves the other's

136
00:10:09,486 --> 00:10:11,442
index describing the old tree.

137
00:10:11,782 --> 00:10:16,862
Committing in the stale worktree. Root cause:
the index still holds the old tree, so

138
00:10:16,902 --> 00:10:20,946
the commit records the inverse of
the commit that moved the branch.

139
00:10:21,286 --> 00:10:25,162
"The branch is not checked out
anywhere, why can I not delete it?"

140
00:10:25,202 --> 00:10:30,086
Root cause: a branch in the middle of a
rebase or bisect in some worktree is in use

141
00:10:30,126 --> 00:10:33,467
by that worktree, although the
list shows it as detached.

142
00:10:35,307 --> 00:10:41,575
Now, out of the lab. A model-evaluation job
runs for forty minutes against the files

143
00:10:41,615 --> 00:10:47,582
of a release tag. With one working tree
nobody can touch the repository until it

144
00:10:47,622 --> 00:10:53,589
finishes. With git worktree add --detach
../rag-api-v1.3.0 v1.3.0 the job gets a

145
00:10:53,629 --> 00:10:56,818
directory of its own,
and you keep working.

146
00:10:57,158 --> 00:11:01,109
The same shape answers the team
lead's question about four agents.

147
00:11:01,149 --> 00:11:06,067
No, you don't need four clones. Four linked
worktrees, each on its own new branch,

148
00:11:06,107 --> 00:11:10,966
share one object database. Each agent has
its own files, its own HEAD and its own

149
00:11:11,006 --> 00:11:15,743
index, and the one-branch rule guarantees
that no two of them can move the same

150
00:11:15,783 --> 00:11:20,762
branch under each other. What they do share,
and must be told about, is the subject

151
00:11:20,802 --> 00:11:21,850
of the next video.

152
00:11:23,690 --> 00:11:30,802
Your turn. Do Exercise 14.1, Level 1, "A
second working tree for a hotfix", in

153
00:11:30,842 --> 00:11:34,836
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

154
00:11:35,176 --> 00:11:40,183
Before you create the worktree, write down
which files and directories will exist in

155
00:11:40,223 --> 00:11:43,428
the new directory and under
.git/worktrees afterwards.

156
00:11:43,468 --> 00:11:48,415
After you commit in the new worktree, predict
what git branch -v shows in the first

157
00:11:48,455 --> 00:11:51,179
one, including the markers,
before you run it.

158
00:11:51,519 --> 00:11:57,350
The challenge is Exercise 14.9, Level 4,
"Two fixes in a worktree that no longer

159
00:11:57,390 --> 00:11:59,258
exists", in the same file.

160
00:12:01,098 --> 00:12:03,029
Answer out loud. I'll wait.

161
00:12:07,869 --> 00:12:12,262
A strong answer has two lists and keeps
them apart: what appears in the new

162
00:12:12,302 --> 00:12:17,228
directory, including the nature of its .git,
and what appears inside the repository,

163
00:12:17,268 --> 00:12:21,543
by file name. It explains the two small
pointer files and what uses them.

164
00:12:21,583 --> 00:12:26,154
It says explicitly what isn't created or
copied. And it connects the layout to

165
00:12:26,194 --> 00:12:31,119
behavior: which state is therefore private,
which is shared, and how you ask Git for

166
00:12:31,159 --> 00:12:33,897
the real path of a file
instead of assuming it.

167
00:12:35,737 --> 00:12:38,664
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

168
00:12:39,004 --> 00:12:44,291
A linked worktree is a second working tree
with its own HEAD, index and HEAD reflog,

169
00:12:44,331 --> 00:12:46,130
on the same objects and refs.

170
00:12:46,470 --> 00:12:52,011
Its .git is a file that points at
.git/worktrees/<id>/ in the repository.

171
00:12:52,351 --> 00:12:57,515
Refs under refs/ are shared, with three
exceptions; root refs such as HEAD are per

172
00:12:57,555 --> 00:13:01,259
worktree; git rev-parse --git-path
tells me which is which.

173
00:13:01,599 --> 00:13:06,492
A branch can be checked out in one worktree
only, because one ref with two indexes

174
00:13:06,532 --> 00:13:07,996
produces a silent revert.

175
00:13:08,336 --> 00:13:13,468
For the files of a branch that is in use
elsewhere, I add a detached worktree or a

176
00:13:13,508 --> 00:13:15,107
new branch, never --force.

177
00:13:16,947 --> 00:13:21,972
Read sections 25.1 to 25.4 of Chapter 25.

178
00:13:22,312 --> 00:13:27,614
Today you saw what a worktree is on disk,
what it shares, and why one branch lives

179
00:13:27,654 --> 00:13:33,022
in one worktree. Before the next video, add
a worktree in the lab and read its .git

180
00:13:33,062 --> 00:13:38,494
file. Next time: worktrees in use, with a
hotfix during a rebase, a review, the life

181
00:13:38,534 --> 00:13:43,446
cycle and the pitfalls. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

182
00:13:43,486 --> 00:13:45,010
See you in the next one.
